Session log -- 2026-05-20 → 2026-05-21

Closes the PhysicalSpec backfill campaign opened by #130 in early
May. Three cross-repo data sprints landed (#62 AMD/Ampere CPU, #68
Intel Xeon, #72 Bucket A long-tail) and three umbrella issues closed
(#130 at 79%, #234 by #235, #241 at 91%). Catalog ceiling reached:
every SKU with public die data is now populated. Only 4 N/A entries
remain, all bounded by data availability (Mali / Snapdragon Ride no
public data; AmpereOne 1-core / DFM-128 synthetic).

Cumulative trajectory: 9/47 (19%) → 43/47 (91%) across the full
campaign. 7 graphs PRs + 14 embodied-schemas PRs merged across the
2-day arc.


WHAT WAS DONE

Day 1 (2026-05-20): close #130 + open #234 + open #241 + start sprint #72

1. Resolve PhysicalSpec wiring sub-issue (#234 / #235)
   - Identified the gap: the post-#185 ComputeProduct YAML-loader
     migration left mapper.physical_spec untouched. 16 mappers were
     loading their HardwareResourceModel from compute_products/
     YAMLs but never reading the die data already present in those
     same YAMLs.
   - Added load_physical_spec_from_compute_product_or_none() in
     physical_spec_loader.py. Reads data/compute_products/<vendor>/
     YAMLs and resolves dies[0].process_node_id against
     data/process-nodes/. Counterpart to load_physical_spec_or_none
     which reads the older data/{gpus,cpus,kpus,npus,chips}/ layout.
   - Wired into 16 mapper factories across CPU/NPU/DSP/TPU/DPU/CGRA
     categories. Coverage: 9/47 → 21/47 (19% → 45%).

2. KPU PhysicalSpec regression fix (#236)
   - Discovered while verifying #235's coverage: the existing 4 KPU
     factories called load_physical_spec_or_none against the legacy
     data/kpus/ path, but the KPU SKU YAMLs had relocated to
     data/compute_products/stillwater/ during the ComputeProduct
     unification (around embodied-schemas#156/#168). Silent fallback
     to physical_spec=None for all 4 KPUs.
   - Switched the factories to the new compute_product loader.
     Coverage 21/47 → 25/47 (45% → 53%).
   - Disclosed in the PR description that #235's "21/47" claim had
     double-counted the broken KPUs.

