KEY DECISIONS

- **Single virtual die approximation for chiplets**. All multi-die
  CPU/Xeon SKUs (EPYC 9654/9754/9965, Xeon 8490H/8592+/6980P) use
  a single virtual die with silicon_bin decomposing across
  chiplets, because the schema's AnyBlock discriminated union has
  no IOBlock kind for the IOD. packaging.num_dies and
  packaging.kind=chiplet remain accurate (4-13 dies depending on
  SKU); the dies[] list is the approximation. Documented in each
  YAML's header + flagged for re-author once an IOBlock kind
  lands. See DECISION-2026-05-21-001.

- **Per-die vs chip-level performance modeling for TPUs**. The
  existing TPU v4 YAML uses a 2-MXU per-die model (matching one
  TensorCore's datapath); chip-level performance roll-up reflects
  Google's marketed per-chip numbers (which include both TCs
  sharing the die). Continued this convention for v3 and v5p;
  TPU Edge Pro is the catalog's only single-MXU TPU model
  (architecturally a scaled-up v1). The modeling gap is
  documented in every TPU YAML's notes; deferred to v8
  multi-block-per-die schema work.

- **Control-mapper rotation**. The
  test_unpopulated_factory_returns_none_physical_spec test (+ 4
  CLI tests) pin a specific SKU as the "unpopulated control" to
  verify graceful degradation. As sprints consumed prior controls,
  the rotation went A100 (original) → T4 → ARM Mali-G78. Each
  rotation preserved the history as inline comments documenting
  why the previous control was retired. This pattern likely
  continues for any future N/A-consuming sprint.

- **Hypothetical SKU treatment**. TPU Edge Pro has no real silicon
  (graphs' research-only model for fair-comparison studies vs
  KPU T256 at 30W). Matched Plasticine v2's convention:
  lifecycle=engineering_sample, model_tier=research,
  is_available=false. Both YAMLs are the catalog's only non-
  shipping entries.

- **Closing #241 at 91% (not 100%)**. The remaining 4 N/A break
  into "no public data exists" (Mali, Snapdragon Ride) and
  "synthetic / no silicon" (AmpereOne 1-core ref, DFM-128).
  Pushing further requires either die-shot reverse-engineering or
  proprietary disclosure -- outside the scope of this work line.
  91% is "everything for which public data exists." Reopening
  criteria documented in the closing comment.


CROSS-REPO WORK PATTERN

Established and now well-exercised: data sprints in embodied-schemas
land one SKU per PR (with parity tests + catalog-count pin bumps),
followed by a consolidated wiring PR in graphs (one per sprint).
Each sprint takes ~6 PRs across the two repos for 5-6 SKUs. CI runs
on both sides catch the catalog drift issues immediately.

This session ran 3 sprints back-to-back: #62 (5 SKUs), #68 (3 SKUs),
#72 (6 SKUs). 14 data PRs + 7 wiring PRs = 21 merged PRs total.


